  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Late last week, the Bureau of Labor Statistics \nreleased its monthly jobs report with some really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4880,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good news. We had added an extra 178,000 jobs in \nthe month before and unemployment had fallen by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modest.1%. But uh this raised the obvious question \nof how could that possibly be true. Consumer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sentiment has hit its lowest level since the data \nwas first collected, beating out the height of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "COVID, the global financial crisis, and even the \noil shock of the late '7s. The Conference Board  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survey found that the highest number of consumers \never considered jobs hard to get. And Indeed's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data team found that overall hiring was in an \nextremely defensive posture amongst the companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they service. Now, perhaps all of these sentiments \ncould be written off as bad vibes amongst  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people who don't truly appreciate how hot this \neconomy is, except that on top of all of this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is a mountain of data that just straight \nup contradicts this number. In March alone, when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we apparently added 178,000 new jobs, companies \nannounced over 60,000 job cuts, the Fed's internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data showed low hiring. Weekly unemployment \nclaims increased to 219,000. And in the exact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same report from the BLS themselves, they noted \nthat the labor force had shrunk by 396,000. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just to make things clear, unemployment claims \nrose, companies cut jobs, and labor force shrunk.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet the official jobs figure went up. The \noverall trust in these figures has been waning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for some time now as massive revisions become \ncommonplace and we swing between massive gains  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and massive losses every single month. Even here \non this channel, I've spent more time than I would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like to admit methodically reverse engineering the \nstatistical technicalities that these government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "departments use to produce their numbers because, \nwell, it's really important to our everyday lives.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in this case, just basic arithmetic should \nprove that something is going wrong here. Either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some combination of these numbers are just plain \nwrong or they are so unintuitive or unreliable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they may as well be disregarded entirely. \nBreaking numbers right now. Economists had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forecasted 65,000 jobs at it. Major rebound for \nAmerican workers after shedding more than 130,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs in February. A blowout jobs number. How much \nmomentum is in this economy? David Intel with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its research and development hub in Hillsboro is \nlaying off workers yet again. Right now, federal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workers are bracing for potential mass layoffs \nas we enter day three of a government shutdown.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146080,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Trump administration's beginning mass layoffs. The \neconomy added 172,000 new private sector jobs to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153840,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kick off the year. The United States added 172,000 \njobs. The private sector. Okay. So, when you read  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a headline or listen to a politician talking \nabout all of the jobs they have just created,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the number they are talking about is almost always \nthe Bureau of Labor Statistics total non-farm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payroll count. It is the number that moves bond \nyields the morning it gets released, the number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that sets the tone for Federal Reserve meetings, \nand the number that politicians on both sides of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the aisle will use to claim that the economy is \neither roaring or collapsing depending on what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suits them that week. The problem is that more and \nmore over time, it has slowly stopped describing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189280,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the thing most people actually mean when they \nhear the word jobs. When the BLS says 178,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs were added, most people hear 178 previously \nunemployed Americans waking up to a new paycheck,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what it actually means is something much, much \nmore complicated and uh significantly less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reassuring. To understand why these numbers have \ndrifted so far away from what they sound like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need to look at where they actually come \nfrom. The Bureau of Labor Statistics doesn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a magic real-time feed of every paycheck in \nthe United States. Despite what you might assume,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2026, with the IRS sitting on a real time \nwithholding database that could in theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell you exactly how many workers got paid in any \ngiven week, that's not the data we use. Instead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every single month, the BLS runs two completely \nseparate surveys. the establishment survey and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "household survey. The former surveys individual \nbusiness sites and offices and gathers data on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how many people they have on their payroll, what \nthose people do for work, and where that job site  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is actually physically located. The latter, as the \nname might suggest, surveys hundreds of thousands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of households to ask them if they lost their \njob, got a job, remained employed, or unemployed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The results of one of them gets printed on \nthe front page of the Wall Street Journal,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the other one gets quietly buried in \nthe back of the same report. Now, logically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a healthy data ecosystem, the two surveys \nshould be moving in roughly the same direction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of the time with small disagreements at the \nmargins. That used to be the case. For most of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the post-war period, the establishment number and \nthe household number tracked each other reasonably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well. When one said the labor market was \nstrong, the other almost always agreed. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the last decade, that relationship has \nvisibly broken down. Economists at the San  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Francisco Fed have published research showing that \nthe gap between the two surveys has widened to its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largest level since they started keeping records, \nand the divergence has accelerated specifically in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the period after 2020. In plain English, the two \nnumbers that are supposed to be measuring the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing are increasingly telling us two completely \ndifferent stories about the American labor market.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the one we have decided to focus on is the \none that structurally is set up to look better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than reality almost every single month. Again, \njust last month, the payroll survey said we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gained 178,000 new jobs, while the back of the \nreport, households claimed to have lost 64,000,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which really just shouldn't be possible. Now, \nyou might be thinking, this is the kind of thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the BLS would be quietly working to fix in the \nbackground. And to their credit, in their own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical notes, they actually emit a lot of these \nproblems. They publish the household number. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "publish the revisions. They publish footnotes on \nmethodology. And they publish their own confidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intervals. The catch is that nobody who isn't \npaid to do this for a living ever reads any of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that. Markets react to the headline. Politicians \nreact to the headline. Business owners deciding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether to hire react to the headline. Workers \ndeciding whether their job is safe react to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "headline. And the headline keeps coming from the \nsurvey that has the worst structural fit for what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the modern American economy actually looks like. \nWhich raises the obvious question, how has this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number become so divorced from reality? And why \ndo we even pay attention to it anymore? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's time to learn how many works to find out what \nthe monthly jobs report is actually measuring,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why it keeps contradicting itself by hundreds of \nthousands of workers in the exact same report,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and why nobody actually wants to fix it. Every \nweek there's another massive data breach,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another AI model scraping personal data, or \nanother company rewriting who owns yours. If that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes you uneasy, you're not alone. And that's \nwhy this video is sponsored by Proton. Proton  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is an endto-end encrypted platform built by CERN \nscientists who wanted a more private internet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over 100 million people use it now, and the free \nplan gets you Proton Mail for secure email and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proton Drive for encrypted cloud storage in one \naccount. What I actually like is how easy it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes privacy. Your emails are encrypted so only \nyou and the recipient can read them. Your files  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are encrypted before they leave your device. And \nbecause Proton is based in Switzerland, your data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is covered by some of the strongest privacy laws \nin the world. You can even create email aliases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cut down on spam and fishing. And Proton Drive \nworks like any other cloud storage, just without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anyone, not even Proton, seeing your stuff. If \nyou want to take control of your digital life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can get Proton Mail and Proton Drive together \nfor free. Check out the link in my description to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get started. Okay, so it is easy and honestly \na little bit understandable to look at these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consistently inaccurate economic metrics and \nconclude that the agencies putting them together  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just trying to make the politicians they \nanswer to look good. However, at the very least,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before we attribute this all to malice, we should \nunderstand how it is much more likely to just be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "statistical stupidity. And to that end, there \nare really three big problems with how we come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with this number. And the first simple problem \nis that payrolls are not people. The establishment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survey does exactly what the name suggests. It \nsurveys establishments and asks them how many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payrolls they process that month. At the time \nthis data source was first conducted, it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a perfectly reasonable proxy for how many people \nhave jobs. One person, one employer, one payroll.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is still the norm for most employees, but \nit's getting less and less normal every year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days, a single person can be attached to \nmultiple payrolls. And the number going up doesn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "necessarily mean a new person got a new job. It \nmight just mean an existing worker picked up a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second one to keep up with rent. The most extreme \nversion of this shows up in healthcare, where it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty common place for doctors and nurses to work \nacross multiple different establishments. A single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doctor working at a hospital, a private practice, \nand doing some research on the side could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technically add three payrolls to the official \nnumber while only being one single working person.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This technicality was particularly important for \nlast month's job numbers because it also counted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "35,000 healthcare professionals returning to work \nafter having been on strike the month before.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545040,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hopefully, it should go without saying that this \nisn't the same thing as the economy genuinely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "providing employment opportunities to 35,000 new \npeople. But all this data does is look at payrolls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every month. So, the establishment survey says \nwe added 178,000 payrolls. The household survey  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the same report on the same day says 64,000 \nfewer humans actually have work. Both of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers are technically true. They are just \nmeasuring different things and almost nobody  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside of macronerds ever bothers to look at the \nsecond one. Which brings us to problem number two.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The methodology. Collecting payroll data at the \nscale of the entire American economy is genuinely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible. And the model the BLS uses to do it \nis becoming increasingly outdated. Payrolls are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not counted firm to firm but rather location \nto location. Again, the establishment in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594000,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "establishment survey means a survey of individual \nwell establishments. So, as more and more people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "move into jobs that are work from home, jobs that \nmove between cities or jobs that run out of a head  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605600,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "office on the other side of the country, it has \nbecome much harder to accurately assign those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payrolls to a real workplace and monitor them \nmonthtomonth. Double counting, undercounting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and plain old misunderstandings about what payroll \nshould count have just become far more common as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our working arrangements have become less rigid. \nThis is part of the reason why revisions have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gotten so embarrassingly large. Last year \nalone, the BLS quietly revised away 800,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jobs that they had previously claimed were added, \nmeaning the headline number for an entire chunk of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the year was just wrong. You can actually go and \nlook up the exact collection methodology that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "BLS uses on their website and you can see it was \ndesigned for a time when most people either went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a factory or an office, did their hours, and \nwent home every day. To try and balance this out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the BLS also uses something called the bursts and \ndeath model, which is essentially a statistical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "estimate of how many businesses they think were \ncreated or shut down in any given month used to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fill in the gaps that the survey itself can't \ncapture. The births and deaths model has its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own problems, but the biggest one is that it is \nheavily skewed by the proliferation of gig work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we have mentioned a few times in previous \nvideos. A new business in this model can be a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guy who started driving for Uber last week. That \ngets counted as a new job creating enterprise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even though it is functionally a person without \nstable employment trying to make rent. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the survey itself misses the modern workforce. \nAnd the model designed to patch the survey is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also calibrated for a workforce that doesn't \nreally exist anymore. Now, these growing blind  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spots in one of the most important metrics in our \neconomy is not great, but it gets worse. The third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is by far the biggest one, and that is \nthe people the payroll survey doesn't see at all.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The establishment survey by its very nature tends \nto count people in traditional payroll jobs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which on average means more secure positions \nwith more established employers who actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "respond to voluntary BLS requests. An increasingly \nlarge share of the American workforce does not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fit any of those descriptions. They are private \ncontractors. And this is not just gig workers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This includes everyone from a guy delivering \ngroceries through Door Dash all the way up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremely high-end technical roles, consultants, \nfreelance software engineers, and well, yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basically the entire creator economy. Can't \nforget about the real heroes. These people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get counted as employed or unemployed by the \nhousehold survey. And if they lose their work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it still sucks just as much as anybody else losing \ntheir job. But they are completely invisible to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payroll. They are also conveniently the people \nmost likely to lose their work before anybody  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "else in more secure formal roles. When a company \nstarts to feel a downturn coming, the first line  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "items to get cut are not the staff with employment \ncontracts and severance packages. The first to go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the contractors, the freelancers, the agency \nthe marketing team was using, the consultants,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the uh inoff life coaches that teach the CEO \nhow to act human. None of those people show up as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layoffs on press releases. None of them show up \nin the establishment survey and it can take weeks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or months before they show up in unemployment \nclaims because most of them aren't eligible to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "file in the first place. This is how the labor \nforce can fall by almost 400,000 people in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single month while payrolls technically still go \nup. The two surveys are just capturing two very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different groups of workers and those groups are \ndrifting further apart every year. One group is on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a W2 in an HR system with their payroll processed \nby a service the BLS already knows how to call.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other is invoicing clients paying their own \ntaxes and disappearing from the data the second  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the contract dries up. Which raises a perfectly \nreasonable question. If the payroll survey is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flawed, why do we still use it? Well, two reasons. \nThe first is that it's fast. It can give us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monthly data with comparatively little collection \nwhich matters for an economy where the Fed and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the government need to make decisions on a near \nreal-time basis. The second is that the payroll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839040,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survey is reliableishly unreliable. Businesses \nare not exactly motivated to make up payrolls to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boost national statistics. Whereas individual \nhouseholds can be koi about their employment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "situation for all kinds of reasons. People who \nhave a job are less likely to answer the phone for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a BLS survey because they are well working. People \nwho just lost one might tailor their response out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of embarrassment or genuine confusion about what \ncounts as actively looking for work. There is also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a less talked about timing problem. The household \nsurvey covers the week containing the 12th of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "month. So, a person who loses their job on the \n13th keeps counting as employed for another 4  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weeks before the data even has a chance to catch \nup. So, the household survey is not some pristine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alternative the BLS is just refusing to use. It \nhas its own real problems. Now, I know this again  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sounds like boring money man rants about data \ncollection methodologies for 15 minutes straight.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this really does matter for two reasons. The \nfirst is that of course these numbers get used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make real decisions that affect us. Federal \nReserve policy on interest rates, stimulus debates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Congress, how foreign investors price treasury \nyields, and ironically, it even feeds into a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business's own plans to hire. If companies see \nunemployment rising and headline payrolls falling,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they become more riskaverse, which leads to less \nhiring, which leads to less consumer spending,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which leads to even less hiring. So, it's just as \nimportant to make sure that these numbers don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look artificially pessimistic as it is to make \nsure they don't look artificially optimistic. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second reason this is so important to understand \nis that if these numbers keep looking like total  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nonsense, it becomes easier for people to dismiss \nthem as useless at best or outright propaganda at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worst. Not having faith in our economic statistics \nis a great way to undermine faith in the economy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a whole. Now, the economists at the BLS know \nall of this. They have written papers about it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Statistics agencies in other developed economies \nhave already started rebalancing. The reason the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "United States hasn't is structural. Changing the \nmethodology would create a one-time discontinuity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the data series, which makes year-over-year \ncomparisons look weird, which the market hates. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would also require the BLS to admit on the record \nthat the number it has been publishing for decades  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn't really measuring what people thought it was, \nwhich is the kind of emission federal agencies do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not love making in front of a Congress that is \nalready gunning to cut their funding. And on top  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all of that, every administration in power \nhas a quiet incentive to leave a politically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convenient noise generator exactly where it is. \nA monthly number that can swing 200,000 jobs in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either direction based on which doctors happen to \nbe on strike that month is incredibly useful if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are the one writing the press release. So the \nnumber stays, the methodology stays, the revisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get larger, and the gap between the headline and \nthe lived experience of the labor market keeps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "widening, which means for the foreseeable future, \nthe most quoted economic statistic in the United  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "States is going to keep telling a story that fewer \nand fewer working people actually recognize. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if all of that wasn't enough, go and watch this \nvideo next to further understand how to lie  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with statistics. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
